Templates, f"{...}", hold their text exactly as written, up to the first }" outside a hole. Quotes,
backslashes, and lone brackets need no escapes, and holes may contain Strings and other templates.

$[Import* std]
$[# who "Ada"]
$[# {greet p} f"{Hello, I am "${.name p}", ${.age p} years old.}"]
${greet {. name "Ada" age 36}}
expect: "Hello, I am \"Ada\", 36 years old."

A backslash is text, $$ is a dollar, and a hole holds a String or another template.
${++ f"{He said "hi" to [me] at \n {braces} $$5, ${str "x"} and ${++ f"{in$who}" "ner"}}" ""}
expect: "He said \"hi\" to [me] at \\n {braces} $5, x and inAdaner"

Unbalanced braces, brackets, and quotes in the text do not end the region around it.
${++ f"{a } b ] c " d {}" ""} ;; a comment
expect: "a } b ] c \" d {"

The one text a template cannot hold, }", goes in a hole.
${++ f"{a ${str "}\""} b}" ""}
expect: "a }\" b"

LaTeX source is text like any other.
${latexSource {latex f"{\frac{$who}{2}}"}}
expect: "\\frac{Ada}{2}"

Hygiene: names in the holes of a template a macro introduces resolve where the macro is defined.
$[# {exclaim s} {++ s "!"}]
$[Macro !hello {{x} {{^ {v} f"{Hello, ${exclaim v}}"} x}}]
${{^ {} [# exclaim 0] {!hello who}}}
expect: "Hello, Ada!"

A template that starts with a newline loses it, the shared indentation, and a final blank line.
${++ f"{
    two
      lines
    }" ""}
expect: "two\n  lines\n"

A template in a lambda, and `f` is still an ordinary name where no `"{` follows it.
$[# f 2]
${map {' 1 2} {^ {n} f"{#$n}"}} ${+ f 1}
expect: ["#1", "#2"]
expect: 3

In prose, a dollar before a template makes it a region of its own: $f"{I am $who.}"
expect: "I am Ada."
